  "facts": [],
  "plan": {
    "decision": "write",
    "reason": "The PR's priority filter is covered for valid, invalid ('urgent') and absent values. Near-miss values are not tested: an empty value, a wrong-case value and a repeated parameter. All three fall outside the documented enum. A looser schema would accept them and send an unintended filter to the repository, and the existing tests would not catch it.",
    "cases": [
      {
        "id": "list-priority-near-miss-422",
        "sourceLocation": "backend/src/features/todos/router.ts:13",
        "behavior": "GET /api/todos rejects priority values outside the exact enum low|medium|high (empty, wrong case, repeated parameter) with 422 and never queries the repository.",
        "requirementSource": "backend/openapi.json in the diff: the priority query parameter has enum [low, medium, high] and the route documents a 422 'Invalid input' response. The validators.ts priority enum is case-sensitive.",
        "inputs": "GET /api/todos?priority= ; GET /api/todos?priority=HIGH ; GET /api/todos?priority=high&priority=low",
        "expected": "Status 422 with a non-empty detail array, and repository.list is not called.",
        "assertion": "it.each over the three query strings. Assert response.status is 422, Array.isArray(response.body.detail) is true with length > 0, and expect(repository.list).not.toHaveBeenCalled().",
        "mockBoundary": "The repository is a vi.fn mock. Express and supertest are real. The zod schema is unmocked.",
        "confidence": "contract-backed",
        "value": {
          "action": "add",
          "testPath": "backend/tests/router.test.ts",
          "existingTests": [
            {
              "path": "backend/tests/router.test.ts",
              "name": "rejects an invalid priority query without calling the repository"
            },
            {
              "path": "backend/tests/router.test.ts",
              "name": "forwards a valid priority query to the repository"
            },
            {
              "path": "backend/tests/router.test.ts",
              "name": "does not forward unrelated query parameters to the repository"
            }
          ],
          "regression": "The query schema is loosened to z.string().optional(), or the empty string, a lowercased value or an array is coerced or ignored. The route then returns 200 and lists tasks with a wrong or missing filter. The existing 'urgent' test still passes.",
          "rationale": "The existing invalid-input test only uses a clearly invalid string. The empty value, case sensitivity and repeated-parameter array are separate mistakes that 'urgent' cannot catch. Cost is one parameterised test using the existing setup."
        }
      }
    ],
    "paths": [
      "backend/tests/router.test.ts"
    ],
    "remainingGaps": [
      "backend/openapi.json is not supplied in full, so the OpenAPI document change (priority parameter, 422 response) cannot be asserted. factory.test.ts stubs the spec file.",
      "Uncovered lines in factory.ts:20, main.ts and the frontend are outside this PR change and were not analysed.",
      "The frontend has no priority-filter change in this PR."
    ]
  },
  "review": {
    "status": "passed",
    "reason": "The added it.each case in backend/tests/router.test.ts runs the real router through Express and supertest. Only the repository is mocked, and the zod listQuerySchema is not. The expected 422 comes from the documented enum low|medium|high, not from copying source output: '' and 'HIGH' are not enum members, and a repeated scalar parameter parses to an array, which z.enum rejects. The router can only return 422 on GET / through the listQuerySchema.safeParse failure branch (router.ts:14-15), so the status reflects the intended branch rather than a setup failure. I checked the planned regressions against concrete mutants. Each would return 200 and call repository.list, so the assertions catch them while the existing 'urgent' test may still pass. The mutants were: loosening to z.string().optional(), lowercasing before the enum check (only 'urgent' still fails), taking the first array element, and treating '' as absent. Together they add distinct protection at the cost of one parameterised test that reuses the existing setup(). I found no material duplication, isolation problem or oracle counterexample.",
    "findings": []
  },
